  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2)/2, drawn as a surface at half the loss, over x and z from -2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so overshoot appears there first. Steps diverge once learning rate x 2.5 > 2, i.e. above 0.8.",
    "Each step: move by -learning rate x (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread 1.5 x sqrt((64 - batch)/(63 x batch)): largest at 1 and exactly zero at 64.",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step (the actual move). The grey arrow is the full-data step, -learning rate x true slope. Both are drawn at true length (scale 1, in bowl units).",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished) or when the ball leaves the drawn bowl or its loss passes 6 (diverged). The trail is the start point plus one point per step.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence. New run, keep last path uses the next noise values and keeps the last run as the ghost.",
    "The loss readout shows the true value. Positions far off the bowl are capped on screen."
  ]
}